{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_bdrk_3wubfftnrp3k4nemtfoozrtziundpu2d4puelqbcf3xlf2kitlxa","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_bdrk_013SiNz3cwPLN7JxpqJG3kt6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Audit C vs Rust translation","subagent_type":"general-purpose","prompt":"You are auditing a C-to-Rust translation for EXACT behavioural equivalence. Do NOT modify any files — this is a read-only audit; report findings only.\n\nWorking directory: /local/home/scheschb/.harvest/work/harvest-work-ZlY6yi/translated_rust\n\nC ground truth: c_src/src/matrix.c, c_src/src/write.c, c_src/src/driver.c, c_src/include/matrix.h, c_src/include/write.h\nRust translation: src/matrix.rs, src/write.rs, src/driver.rs, src/cffi.rs, src/lib.rs\n\nRead every file completely, then compare STATEMENT BY STATEMENT. For each C function, list the C statements and the corresponding Rust statements and judge equivalence on an x86-64 Linux/glibc target (LP64: int=32-bit, size_t=64-bit, char=signed).\n\nFocus specifically on these classes of subtle divergence:\n1. Integer conversions: `int` → `size_t` in malloc arguments (sign extension), signed overflow/wrapping in `buffer_size = mat->height * (mat->width * 10 + mat->width) + mat->height + 1`, `sizeof` values (sizeof(matrix_t)=16, sizeof(int*)=8, sizeof(int)=4).\n2. Exact error message strings (byte-for-byte, including punctuation, newlines, and format specifiers) and which stream/function emits them (perror vs fprintf(stderr,...)).\n3. Return values / sentinels: NULL vs non-NULL, EINVAL=22, EXIT_FAILURE=1, EXIT_SUCCESS=0, `return errno` read at exactly the same point relative to intervening calls (fprintf/fclose can clobber errno).\n4. Order of operations and side effects: which frees happen on each error path, the order of malloc/free calls, loop bounds and off-by-one (`j <= i` in the allocate_matrix cleanup loop, `j < mat->width - 1` separator condition), pointer arithmetic.\n5. Missing checks or ADDED checks: the Rust must NOT add safety checks the C does not have (e.g. C's initialize_matrix_from_string does not check whether allocate_matrix returned NULL; C's multiply_matrices does not check its arguments for NULL).\n6. Anything in the C source that has no counterpart in the Rust source, or vice versa (extra/missing functions, different signatures, different struct layout).\n7. snprintf buffer size (C uses `char buffer[12]` and `sizeof(buffer)`).\n\nReport a concise table of findings: for each C function, either \"EQUIVALENT\" with a one-line justification, or a precise description of the divergence (file:line in both languages, what C does, what Rust does, and a concrete input that would expose it). Be rigorous and skeptical; also flag anything you are unsure about."}}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":834,"cache_read_input_tokens":157095,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":834,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":2,"service_tier":"standard"},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"0ac7619d-9520-4925-a201-0b56279db839","uuid":"a5ce6a7e-3bcc-4312-8971-23182eaace4f","timestamp":"2026-08-19T21:44:20.877Z"}
